			<div class="content" id="post-content-3768438">
									

<p>I enjoy this, and that's a statement I'm finding myself making less and less these days.</p>
<p>Just because something is simple does not mean that it is bad, nor does it mean that there isn't a <em>story being told,</em> just because it's not spelled out with prose or slapped across the reader's face.</p>
<p>It might look like a &quot;Thing What Does a Thing,&quot; but it does that <em>well:</em> it's not ridiculously over-the-top, but neither does it just sit there existing and performing some <em>task.</em> The bird, apparently, doesn't want to be touched; if it wants, is aware of itself, then it's some form of &quot;alive.&quot; That alone poses enough of a question for me: what made this bird, literally made by the touch of a person, completely reject all further contact? It's damaged, it's been hurt, and it doesn't want to be hurt again.</p>
<p>djkaktus called it a &quot;biting dog&quot; in chat, and that's as good an analogy as any I'd make. Author, please don't be discouraged by the (possible) poor performance of this, and keep at it. I think I'd like to see more from you.</p>

							</div>

								
